{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mr. Santo Napoli stated ok, thank you.  He stated that we will start with comments from the dais on Bill 20.  He asked if Ms. Gerlach prefers to start out with the administration.  It doesn't matter.  He stated that he will start with the administration.  He stated Mr. Kane, our Managing Director, go ahead.\par
\par
Mr. Frank Kane stated so we make big decisions here that affect the lives of the people who live in this community for years or maybe decades after we all leave these positions and Data Centers, and how we treat them, where we locate them in the safeguards that we put in place to make sure that they operate safely, quietly, and in harmony with our neighborhoods. Are exactly one of those decisions.  Last week, we had the privilege of listening to dozens of people who are passionate about this issue and who looks to us to make the best decisions to keep these communities safe while meeting the planning, while meeting the requirements of the Pennsylvania Municipalities Planning Code.  Our residents should know that if you take the time to come out and be heard, we will listen to your comments with an open mind.  He stated that he would like to frame the issue and answer many of the comments that were made in the interest of us making good decisions here tonight.  This body has it before this evening two courses of action tat it could choose between.  On one had, you have the option to vote to act on Bill 20 which strengthens our Zoning Code and creates a special exception designation for Data Centers.  It creates an expanded setback, adds additional layers of safeguards that includes an Environmental Review and Hearing for the Planning Board and places additional requirements on developers who seek to locate Data Centers in our city.  There is concern that from some Bill 20 doesn't go far enough.  Perhaps it doesn't, but it is something.  It creates a process for a much more exhausted review and begins to provide some safeguards for an application submitted to locate a Data Center in our city tomorrow.  The other mechanism that you have available to you is to initiate a Curative Amendment which is a Municipal Curative Amendment which self declares what Councilperson Gerlach just read it.  It self declares that we recognize our Zoning Code is invalid in this area and give us 180 days to initiate in passing the amendment to our own Zoning Code.  Our Council, in this city administration thinks that this option is fraught with unknowns and risks that could result in even more unwanted development.  It would likely take the decision making power out of the hands of City Council and the community and put it in the hands of lawyers and judges.  In our research, no community of our size and no community in the Lehigh Valley has followed this process.  The handful of communities that have are small, rural communities that don't really compare to the city of Allentown.  We are the third largest city in Pennsylvania.  Philadelphia, Pittsburgh , Scranton, Harrisburg, Bethlehem and Reading.  He stated all the cities which they could compare have not followed that approach.  There are some of us that just don't want Data Centers at all.  He stated ever to be located in Allentown, but that train has left the station.  He stated we are required whether we amend our Zoning Ordinance or not to permit this use somewhere.  We can't pretend to permit it.  We can't put so many restrictions on it that it is prohibitive in practice.  It's not in law.  We have to create a reasonable scenario.  That is what we are required to do by law.  So there has been five broad areas of public concern with Bill 20 that have been brought to our attention through  public comment.  First is the setbacks.  A setback is how far inside the property line improvement can be located, must be located.  Bill 20 calls for 200 feet which is one of the largest setbacks in our Code.  We got two hundred feet from the setback from mining operations.  In the interest of brevity, but we could consider and there were talks of a thousand foot setback.  It doesn't make sense for our community.  We couldn't identify one place where there was a thousand foot setback.  That would make sense, but if you will enact Bill 20, we could quickly amend to go to 500 which is the best that they think they could do.  It is important to note that Bill 20 would explicitly call Data Centers, a Special Exception Use which means no use could be allowed without a Public Hearing from the Zoning Hearing Board with feedback in review from the Planning Commission and Environmental Commission.  That is a new feature in Bill 20.  The second broad concern was Noise, we think that our Noise Ordinance already provides revenue from any noise that could be heard beyond property lines and if we need more specific Data Center regulations, it is an easy matter of two weeks to amend our Noise Ordinance and the Bill 20 also specifically calls for a Sound Study before and six months after the use is established.    The Sound Study must assess persistent, total sound, hums, and other low frequency or ambient noise and identifiable recommended mitigation measures.  Third, was disposal and decommissioning.  The Bill specifically calls for, Bill 20 calls for Electronic Waste Plan outlining procedures for safe removals and recycling  or disposal of server infrastructure, hazardous materials, batteries, electronic waste and related projects/products which will apply in cases where the Data Center is updated or decommissioned, but these must be considered before any use is approved.  Fourth:  we permit this use in two zoning areas right now in Bill 20, IG and IM.  We want to make sure this Bill can survive a legal challenge.  When defining a setback buffer, the result must reasonably allow this use in the city and Bill 20 would allow Data Centers in IG and IM Zoning Districts through special exception approval by the Zoning Hearing Board.  This includes approximately 13 to 38 out of Allentown's 34,000 give or take parcels which is less then .1 percent of the parcels in Allentown.  If we limited to IM, only six to 14 parcels would meet the setback requirements so we are concerned that we are open to exclusionary zoning challenges.  Fifth:  an Environmental Plan to review the impact of use on utilities and effective rate payers for water and electric was one of the things that was brought up.  After six hours of public comment and 100 or so personal hours of work, two hours of executive sessions, and three packs of nicotine gum for me, and countless conversations with interested parties, here is what the administration recommends, if you want to provide the most protection and safeguards for the city of Allentown as we look to thoroughly vet proposed Data Centers, the administration urges you to look and go to the affirmative and pass Bill 20 this evening and quickly seek to add further safeguards through follow-up amendments.  Why do we recommend this.  Why not a Curative Amendment?  Why not a Municipal Curative Amendment?  Bill 20 has the affect of a Curative Amendment in the sense that it outlines the process by processes by which we will consider Data Centers.  Bill 20 is the product that you can reasonably expect to come up with after the 180 days that you give yourself cure.  Our counsel, our attorney who advises multiple municipalities of their Zoning Code who has 30 years of experience, has advise us if you did not pass Bill 20 then none of the safeguards in this Bill will be in  place for the next round of Data Centers applications that could be made and we are aware of a few.     You can pass the 180 day Municipal Curative Amendment, but during the 180 day, we can have Data Centers make applications and we will be required to review those applications, if it went to court.  So everyone in this process wants more safeguards, right.  The public here today attends, the members of Council, the administration, we all want the same thing, but to not vote to support Bill 20 is to make the perfect enemy of the good.  There is also a Commonwealth Court Decision Galbreath v Supervisors of Northampton Township and part of the finding of that  decision was that in the event that a Landowners Curative Amendment is brought, the municipality may not undertake a Municipal Curative Amendment.  We have a Curative Amendment proposed for a Landowner already in which you are already aware.  If you seek a different Curative Amendment rather than an Amendment that Bill 20, you will spend a lot of time without the dash, you know.  In the interest of not rambling and basically you will be leaving this community unprotected for six months in order to get a few hundred more feet of setbacks and include some language about noise which you will come up with the after a Curative Amendment.  There are things we can do more easily, more quickly if you pass Bill 20 and we just quickly amend so it brings to mind during the Vietnam War, that was that old adage, we had to destroy the village in order to liberate it.  He urged them not to destroy Allentown's ability to protects itself in order to come up with a better amendment in the next six months.     \par
\par
Mr. Santo Napoli stated alright.  Thank you and we will take comments from the dais.  \par
\par
Mr. Frank Kane stated can he also mention that they have Jill Seitz from the Lehigh Valley Planning Commission here.  She is here to be able to answer questions.  \par
}

