\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1\kerning2\f0\fs24 Mr. Lewis Shupe stated thank you,  Lewis Shupe, Pocono Summit/Allentown, PA.   He stated that he come to talk about flock. Now.    A few years ago, everybody here on council voted for this, despite my misgivings at that time, to ask that it be tabled. It was not. The bill was passed. And here we are three years later at. The devices have already been installed at various choke points throughout the city of Allentown and sensitive locations such as women's health centers, bars and voting poll places and such, and all sorts of other places where these flock devices gather data. Some say they gather Bluetooth and WIFI. I have my doubts about that, but they do capture the license plate data as well as pictures of the vehicle and increasing AI that is capable of hallucinating without chemical assistance, gathers all these data points up into what are called patterns of life. And this is uploaded to places like the fusion center at seventh and Hamilton, where it is disseminated to the NSA and about 50 other three letter agencies. This data has already been proven dangerous to Spanish people, to people of color, to people that the AI thinks might be an illegal immigrant and has already contributed to many people being detained over that. It's been abused by law enforcement looking for ex-partners. All this evidence I've been posting on his Facebook group, Allentown, PA community, everybody here should have gotten an invite. I don't know if he joined or not, but you don't have to. It's an open group. All you have to do is look maybe sort a little bit by flock, which is a dedicated topic. I would ask that you seriously consider getting rid of these cameras. While the legal winds are good. Today, Governor Shapiro came out and claimed that he would be in favor of legislation that would ban Flock cameras in the state of Pennsylvania. So me, in my small data farm that I still have at 11th and Hamilton started spreading the word around saying, hey, maybe if we can get the governor to have an executive order to block this, this might work out better for him and for everybody in general. So I spread around his phone numbers, and I might have his cell phone number later. I'll have to check that. But the point being, these cameras doing a lot of damage to people against their fourth amendment rights, which, by the way, has a defect. I talked about that before, but that's academic. The point being, please start the process to get rid of these cameras while the legal winds are blowing. Good. Thank you.\par
Mr. Santo Napoli stated thank you.\par
Ms. Genesis Ortega stated Kyle Ropski.\par
Mr. Kyle Ropski stated good evening everybody. My name is Kyle and I live at 528 North Burke street. I'm chair of Allentown's Environmental Advisory Council. I just want to come up here again tonight to raise some attention and awareness to the upcoming opportunities to engage, provide input to the city's first ever climate action plan.   We're moving on to the next phase of community forums, where the city will share what it has heard so far in its first round of engagement, and begin discussing specific strategies and actions that are hopefully going to be included in the next or the final plan.    There will be an eastside forum on Wednesday, September 9th from 6 to 7:30 p.m. at the Eastside Youth Center and another in center city on Saturday, October 3rd from 2:00 to 3:30 pm, at the Allentown Public Library. There are also opportunities to participate online anytime through surveys and other pilot program forums, so I encourage everyone to learn more and take part in this plan. You can find the schedule, registration information and updates at allentownpa.gov/climateactionplan. You can also reach out to me or anybody on the environmental advisory council at atownec@gmail.com or our website, allentown.org. And also, just to cherry on top, for those attending in person, $25 stipends are available for your time with registration required. So again, if you know anybody, if they show up to one of these public meetings and they register beforehand, they can get $25 cash for going. So definitely a big incentive and ultimately, again, the plan is to be rooted in community engagement. We want to know what the residents of Allentown think, and that's how bad we want it. So please, if you know anybody, tell them to come out to some of these events. They can make a little money.   It can also really help out the city in the long term. Thank you. \par
